course: english
lesson: 46
title: Poetry: simile, metaphor, personification, alliteration
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Poets paint pictures with words, and they have a few favourite tricks: the simile, the metaphor, personification and alliteration.
explain: A simile compares two things using "like" or "as" ("The sun is like a golden coin"); a metaphor says one thing IS another ("The sun is a golden coin"), not as a lie but to make you see what they share: round, shiny, gold.
explain: Personification gives a human action or feeling to something that is not human: the wind screams, the moon watches, the eagle has hands.
explain: Alliteration repeats the first sound of words close together, like "slithering snakes slide silently", where the hissing s even sounds like the snake; onomatopoeia is different: a word that sounds like what it means, such as buzz, bang or splash.
explain: Picture Kai in Liverpool writing "The wind's screamin' through the streets, like": the "like" at the end is just Scouse filler (Scouse is the Liverpool way of talking), so the trick there is personification, not a simile.
fact: A simile compares two things using "like" or "as": "as brave as a lion", "like a golden coin".
fact: A metaphor says one thing is another for effect, such as "The city is a beast at night"; it is not meant literally, and it is not a lie.
fact: Everyday talk is full of metaphors: "a heart of gold", "a night owl", "my room is a pigsty".
fact: Personification gives human actions or feelings to non-human things: "the wind screamed", "the moon smiled".
fact: Alliteration is the same first sound repeated in words close together: "big brown bear", "slithering snakes slide silently".
fact: Onomatopoeia is a word that sounds like its meaning, such as buzz, hiss, crash and pop; "buzz" on its own is not alliteration.
fact: Tennyson's poem "The Eagle" (public domain) says "He clasps the crag with crooked hands": alliteration of the hard c sound, and personification, since an eagle has talons, not hands.
fact: Good readers name the effect, not only the trick: the hard c sounds make the eagle seem strong, and the "hands" make it seem almost human.
fact: "As cold as ice" is a cliché, a comparison used so often it has gone stale; a fresh one, like "as cold as a metal slide in January", makes a clearer picture.
fact: In Boston and New England "wicked" means "very", so "wicked good" means very good; in Liverpool a "like" at the end of a sentence is a filler word, not a comparison.
fact: More similes do not make a better poem: a few strong ones beat a crowd of weak ones.
fact: When you personify the moon, one or two human actions (it watches, it whispers) work better than giving it a job and a car.
exercise: "The sun is like a golden coin." Which device is this? | A simile | A metaphor | Personification | Alliteration
exercise: "The sun is a golden coin." Ahmed says, "That's a lie, the sun's a star." What is the line really? | A metaphor showing what the sun and a coin share | A lie that good poets should never write | A simile, because it compares the sun and a coin | A mistake, since a poem may only state facts
exercise: Kai writes, "The wind's screamin' through the streets, like." What device is the screaming wind? | Personification | A simile | Onomatopoeia | Alliteration
exercise: Why is Kai's "like" at the end not a simile? | It is Scouse filler and compares nothing | Similes need "as" and can never use "like" | Similes must come at the start of a line | Wind can never be compared with anything
exercise: "Slithering snakes slide silently." Which device is this? | Alliteration | Onomatopoeia | A simile | A metaphor
exercise: Why do the s sounds work in that line? | They hiss, so the sound echoes the snake | Every good poem must use plenty of s | They make the line longer, which earns marks | They make the ends of the lines rhyme
exercise: Ali asks, "Is 'buzz' alliteration?" What is it? | Onomatopoeia: it sounds like its meaning | Alliteration, as it has two z letters | A simile comparing a bee with a noise | A metaphor saying a bee is a noise
exercise: In "He clasps the crag with crooked hands" (Tennyson's "The Eagle"), which devices are used? | Alliteration and personification | A simile and onomatopoeia | A metaphor and a simile | Alliteration, and no other device
exercise: Why does Tennyson give the eagle "hands"? | To make it seem strong and almost human | Because eagles really do have hands | Because he forgot the word "talons" | Because "hands" rhymes with the next line
exercise: Ella says, "The city is a beast at night." Which device is this? | A metaphor | A simile | Alliteration | Onomatopoeia
exercise: A mum in Boston says, "This poem's wicked good with all them similes." What does she mean? | It is very good | It is evil but clever | It is a bit rubbish | It is spooky and dark
exercise: Year 4 must make the moon a person. Which line uses personification? | The moon watched over the sleeping town. | The moon was big and round and white. | The moon is like a shiny silver plate. | The moon shines because sunlight hits it.
exercise: What is the trouble with "as cold as ice"? | It is a cliché, worn out from overuse | It says the thing is really made of ice | It shows that the poet hates the winter | It is not a comparison of any kind
exercise: Which is a fresh simile instead of "as cold as ice"? | As cold as a metal slide in January | As cold as ice cubes in a freezer | As cold as anything ever could be | As cold as snow on a winter's day
exercise: Which of these is NOT a simile? | My room is a pigsty. | He ran like the wind. | She was as quiet as a mouse. | The pond shone like glass.
exercise: Do more similes make a better poem? | No, a few strong ones beat many weak ones | Yes, more similes always score more marks | Yes, every line of a poem needs a simile | No, because good poems never use any
test: Your mate says, "Metaphors are just lies, innit, the sun ain't a coin." Put him right. | It shows a likeness; nobody means it literally | He is right, so poets should stick to facts | He is right, but only for poems about the sun | It is true, since the sun is made of gold
test: Kenzie, Gen Alpha: "'The thunder growled' is a simile, no cap." ("No cap" means no lie.) What is it really? | Personification | A simile | Alliteration | A metaphor about noise
test: What would happen to "The sun is a golden coin" if you put "like" after "is"? | It would become a simile | It would become alliteration | It would become personification | It would stop being poetry at all
test: A cartoon teacher says "Boom!" is alliteration. Fix it. | "Boom" is onomatopoeia: it sounds like its meaning | "Boom" is a metaphor for a big explosion | "Boom" is the personification of a noise | The cartoon is right, it is alliteration
test: Which line uses alliteration? | Pete's purple parrot pecked a pear. | The parrot was like a rainbow. | The parrot laughed loudly at the tabby cat. | The parrot squawked: screech!
test: "Shut up, the wind can't scream, it ain't got a mouth," snaps Ryan. What is the poet doing? | Personifying the wind to show it is wild | Making a science mistake about the wind | Using onomatopoeia for the wind's noise | Writing a simile about wind and mouths
test: Gran in Cardiff: "That poem's lush, bach." ("Lush" means lovely; "bach" is a fond Welsh word, like "dear".) Which line from it is a simile? | The waves rolled in like grey horses. | The waves were grey horses tonight. | The waves whispered to the sand. | Wild waves washed the windy wall.
test: Ella's poem has twenty similes in eight lines. What would a teacher most likely say? | Keep the best few so each one stands out | Brilliant, the more similes the better | Add twenty more to make it really strong | Swap every one of them for a cliché
test: Which pair is matched correctly? | buzz: onomatopoeia; "heart of gold": metaphor | buzz: alliteration; "heart of gold": simile | buzz: simile; "heart of gold": personification | buzz: metaphor; "heart of gold": onomatopoeia
test: In "The moon smiled down on the silver sea", which devices are used? | Personification and alliteration | A simile and onomatopoeia | A simile, and no other device at all | Onomatopoeia and nothing else
test: "The leaves danced like dizzy dancers." Which of simile, personification and alliteration does it use? | All three of them | Only the simile | Only the alliteration | The simile and alliteration, not personification
exam: Identify the literary device whereby two dissimilar entities are compared through the explicit use of the words "like" or "as". | The simile | The metaphor | Personification | Alliteration
exam: Select the response that most accurately characterises the line "The city is a beast at night." | A metaphor, not intended literally | A simile, owing to its comparison | An instance of onomatopoeia | A literal statement of fact
exam: It is given that a poet attributes human actions to an inanimate object (a thing that is not alive). State the name of the device so employed. | Personification | Onomatopoeia | The extended simile | Alliteration
exam: Ascertain which of the following constitutes onomatopoeia rather than alliteration. | Buzz | Big brown bear | Slithering snakes slide | Crooked crag
